## Findings

**P3-1 — `guarded` cannot guard on Dart; corpus runners abort at the first failing case.**
`test/dao/jing/cbor_test.cljc:77-83`. On Dart a failing `is` throws (cljd.test `is` → package:test `expect`, which throws `TestFailure`), so when a case body's `is` fails inside `guarded`, the catch handler's own `(is false …)` throws in turn and escapes — the `doseq` aborts and every later case in that deftest is hidden. The prompt's test criterion 5 asks whether this is "handled everywhere, including decode runners": it is not. Only the two `is false` paths of the encode-refusal runner (`:not-constructible`, `:host-merged`) were converted to collect-and-assert-once (`cbor_test.cljc:114-149`, correct). Still aborting at first failure on Dart: `canonical-cases-encode-and-round-trip` (`:95-111`), `decode-refusal-cases…` (`:152-159`), `groups-hold-on-produced-bytes` (`:162-179`), `encoding-ignores-ambient-print-settings` (`:266-279`), and the refusal-class `is` branches inside encode-refusal (`:133-134`, `:146-147`). Impact is diagnostic only — a mutation still turns the lane red at the first bad case; there is no false-green path. Smallest fix: mirror the encode-refusal pattern — have `guarded` `swap!`-conjure `[id e]` into a shared atom and assert it empty at the end of each runner deftest (or in one trailing deftest), instead of calling `is` inside the catch.

**P3-2 — the two E1 Dart "N/A (merged)" rows are probe-observed, not pinned by any standing test.**
`test/dao/jing/cbor_test.cljc:47-57` with the skip at `:139-143`. The runner is correctly two-sided — a listed-but-not-merged case still asserts the frozen refusal, an unlisted-but-merged case fails the final assert — so the seven "live, refused" rows of the proposed Dart column are pinned by the green lane. But the *merged* half of `coll/set-signed-zero-collapse` and `coll/set-vector-list-collapse` rests on the implementer's probe and the printed SKIP lines; if ClojureDart later holds both members, the tests stay green while the errata's Dart column misstates. This matches ratified J1 practice (the E1 JVM N/A rows were equally observational), so it is non-blocking; the errata sentence is E1's "the tests skip a case only when it is in this table's N/A column for the host and the host really merged its members". Smallest fix, mirroring the `:cljs` evidence test at `:182-192`: a 3-line `:cljd` deftest asserting `(= 1 (count #{0.0 -0.0}))` and `(= 1 (count #{[1] (fresh-list-ish [1])}))` so the column's claim fails loudly if the host changes.

## What I checked and found clean

1. **JVM/Node non-regression.** Every JVM/CLJS adapter is an exact identity of what J1 passed to Boring: `null`→nil, `bool`/`text`/`byte-string`/`integer`→the argument itself (with `check-text`/`copy-range`/`b->int` still applied at the call sites, `boring.cljc:58-107`); `array-node`→the vector; `map-node` is character-for-character J1's inline `(into {} …)` (`cbor.cljc:941-946`); `identifier-wire`'s `array-node`/`text`/`null` wrappers collapse to exactly J1's `[ns-or-nil name]` vector (`:980-988`). No input — integer widths, bignums, nested metadata, empty collections, negative zero — can change bytes on JVM or Node: encode-path code is value-identical, and the reader-side changes (`host-big?` in `length-of`/`arg->big`, the `[tag-or-nil inner raw]` triple at `:1202-1208`, `:1334-1368`) only affect eight-byte-argument paths, preserving the `:unknown-tag`/truncated refusal classes (the old JVM `(long tag)` wrap is gone; no corpus case pins an 8-byte tag, and `(case nil …)` reaches the existing default). The green JVM (4447 assertions) and Node (43538) runs corroborate.
2. **Dart writer, verified against the pinned `cbor` 6.5.1 source** (`~/.pub-cache/hosted/pub.dev/cbor-6.5.1`): shortest heads for every major type including the 8-byte cases — `EncodeSink.addHeaderInfo` branches on `bitLength` (≤23 immediate, ≤8/16/32 byte forms, else `u64BytesHelper`, big-endian, correct for values in [2³², 2⁶³)); negative `CborSmallInt` writes major 1 over `~value`; `CborInt`'s factory sends [2⁶³, 2⁶⁴) BigInts to `_LargeInt`, which writes 8-byte heads (never bignum tags inside the 64-bit range), and ≥2⁶⁴ magnitudes never reach the package at all (`int-wire` routes them through `wire/tagged` with minimal `magnitude-bytes`, no leading zero, empty impossible). Tags 2/3/4/30/27/258 all shortest (`c2/c3/c4/d81e/d81b/d90102`); tag-258 sets and tag-27 frames are definite `CborList`s in insertion order; `CborMap.fromIterables` iterates a Dart `LinkedHashMap` in insertion order, so the adapter's own `sort-by` hex order (lexicographic on lowercase hex ≡ unsigned bytewise, proper prefix first) governs — the package does not re-order, and package `==` cannot silently merge two keys that weren't already refused as duplicate canonical bytes. `CborString` writes strict definite UTF-8; the lone-surrogate→`?` path is unreachable because `check-text` refuses first on every text-bearing path (values, identifier components, metadata). No double ever reaches the package (floats are frame byte strings), so narrowing/re-encoding is impossible; `cborEncode` adds no self-describe tag; the cyclic-reference check can't false-positive (no shared nodes in a wire tree).
3. **Dart host traps in the shared reader/profile.** `BigInt.compareTo` normalized to −1/0/1 (`bcmp`, `cbor.cljc:171-179`) — required by `num-compare`'s tested −1/0/1 contract, and harmless at the other call sites. The Dart SDK decoder does discard a leading U+FEFF (verified in `dart-sdk/lib/convert/utf.dart`) and is strict for overlong/surrogate/>U+10FFFF, so the BOM-restoration loop (`:1090-1107`) is both necessary and correct, including BOM-only and repeated-BOM inputs; non-leading BOMs are content on every host. Surrogate detection via `codeUnitAt` is correct for Dart's UTF-16 strings, with `count` in code units, consistent with the JVM/CLJS arms. All Dart int arithmetic stays in range (4-byte head args < 2³², `hex-int` < 2³², `need`'s `pos+n` bounded by the 2⁵³−1 length clamp). Float bits go through `ByteData` with big-endian defaults; NaN is canonicalized before any `setFloat64`. Keyword/symbol equality on Dart is field-based, so `built` never raises `host-collapse` — pinned by the slash-crossed set test expecting `::none` on `:cljd` (`:195-202`) and the unskipped `coll/map-both-slash-keywords`. `fresh-list` clears ClojureDart's constructor metadata in the fixtures and `frame->value` clears decoded lists (`:1270-1277`); with-meta targets are validated bare, and the green corpus meta/* cases prove `mapv`/`into` products carry no constructor metadata on Dart.
4. **Ratified constraints.** Decimal window `[-2147483647, 2147483648]` is the shared `decimal` check plus the host-independent test table matching E3's four hex rows; depth 128 counts identically on both paths (`:unsupported-value` encode / `:malformed-cbor` decode, 10 000-deep payloads refused, not stack-overflowed); float64 kind preserved (Dart int/double are distinct types and floats never narrow); `BigInt`/carriers for exact integers, decimal, rational — lossy constructors (`(ratio 1.5 2)`, `(decimal 0 2.5)`) refused; explicit outer `clojure/with-meta` frame around lists and symbols; Jing-owned structural validation before any package materialization (only `cborEncode` is referenced; the package decoder is never used); decode re-encodes and byte-compares. One noted non-defect: `exact` on an extreme-window decimal allocates a ~2³¹-digit BigInt under `num=` only — identical on all hosts, unreachable from encode/decode, a ratified-window consequence (E5-adjacent, recorded not changed).
